CORE
type_pack_arguments.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member typedefs: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ partial specialization pattern: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.variadic]/5: a pack-expansion call argument whose pattern names
a type template parameter pack -- `declval<Args>()...', `Args()...',
`static_cast<Args>(0)...' -- expands to one argument per element, also for
an empty pack, in a member typedef of a class template and in a partial
specialization pattern.  On bin53 the argument stayed a single argument
(CONVERSION ERROR here).  Not covered: an EMPTY trailing pack matched
against `probe<decltype(g(declval<Args>()...)), Args...>' (probe<count_of<>>
selects the primary template).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept the program.
